Lemon meringue pie
4
Lemon tart with shortcrust pastry, bain-marie lemon curd and Italian meringue.

---
Pastry
wheat flour|250|g
powdered sugar|75|g
soft butter|125|g
water|38|ml
egg yolk|1|pcs
fine salt|1|pinch

Lemon curd
lemons|2|pcs
powdered sugar|100|g
eggs|110|g
soft butter|100|g

Italian meringue
egg whites|3|pcs
powdered sugar|180|g
water|50|ml

---
Preheat the oven to 180°C.
Make the pastry: mix flour, salt and sugar. Rub in the butter until sandy.
Add the egg yolk and water, bring together without overworking.
Form a ball, roll out to a disc and rest in the fridge at least 20 min.
Line a buttered tart tin, then chill again 30 min.
Bake the case blind 10 min with weights then 5 to 10 min without, until lightly coloured.

Make the lemon curd: juice the lemons.
In a bowl, mix lemon juice, eggs, sugar and butter in pieces.
Cook the curd in a bain-marie stirring until thick (about 82°C in the centre).
Strain through a fine sieve, blend if needed to smooth.
Pour the curd into the cooled case and set in the fridge about 30 min.

Make the Italian meringue: put the whites in the mixer bowl.
Cook the water and sugar to 115°C.
When the syrup is near 100°C, start beating the whites on high.
Pour the hot syrup in a thin stream onto the whites with the mixer on low, then increase and beat until cool.

To serve: pipe the meringue onto the cold tart in peaks.
Brown the meringue with a blowtorch or under the grill.

---
Do not overwork the pastry so it stays short.
Scrape the sides well while cooking the curd to avoid egg coagulation.
The curd is done when it coats the spoon and reaches about 82°C.

---
dessert
tart
lemon
meringue
---
Transport|Delicate
Réchauffage|No
Froid|Keep chilled
Manger|Cutlery
